 The possible lengths for the third side of a triangle must satisfy the triangle inequality theorem, which states that the sum of the lengths of any two sides of a triangle must be greater than the length of the third side. For a scalene triangle with sides of lengths 3 cm, 5 cm, and an unknown side \(x\), the following must be true: \[
\begin{cases}
3 + 5 > x \\
3 + x > 5 \\
5 + x > 3
\end{cases}
\] Simplifying these inequalities, we get: \[
\begin{cases}
8 > x \implies x < 8 \\
x > 2 \\
x > -2 \quad (\text{which is always true if } x > 0)
\end{cases}
